Context

During security testing and review using Burp Suite, it was discovered that the Strict-Transport-Security header is missing from some responses — specifically in requests routed through conditional blocks inside our NGINX configuration (e.g., for .well-known/*, /b2c/mslogin/*, etc.).

This occurs because add_header directives in NGINX do not apply from includes placed outside if blocks when the final response is processed inside the if. To ensure consistent application of security headers, we are moving the include conf.d/common_response_headers.include; into each if block that handles a response.

⸻

List of Changes
	•	Updated nginx_config files across all SPA projects to:
	•	Explicitly include conf.d/common_response_headers.include inside any if block that handles requests directly (e.g., by setting headers or content-type).
	•	Ensure all add_header directives within common_response_headers.include use the always flag.
	•	Reviewed all location blocks across deployed NGINX configs to confirm header consistency.
	•	Added temporary X-Debug-Served-By header to support debugging response origin during QA testing.

⸻

Impact Analysis
	•	Security Hardening: This change ensures Strict-Transport-Security, X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options, and other security headers are applied consistently across all HTTP responses, regardless of logic inside if blocks.
	•	No Functional Change: The actual routing and SPA behavior remain unchanged. Only header behavior is affected.
	•	Slight Increase in Config Redundancy: Some duplication of include statements occurs, but this is necessary due to NGINX’s add_header scoping behavior.
	•	Prepared for Audit: This fix directly addresses a known security audit finding and ensures compliance with OWASP Secure Headers best practices.

⸻

